$ hoarddb restore <connection flags> --bogus
exit: 1
--- stdout ---
--- stderr ---
usage: hoarddb restore [flags]
flag provided but not defined: -bogus
Usage of hoarddb restore:
  -address string
    	hoardDB server address (TCP driver port). Without it the CLI dials the tcp.listen address of the loaded configuration (env HOARDDB_TCP_LISTEN; 127.0.0.1:7433 when nothing is configured), and this default only if that is empty (default "localhost:7433")
  -allow-incomplete
    	restore from a manifest marked incomplete
  -auth-token string
    	AUTH resumption token (env: HOARDDB_AUTH_TOKEN)
  -config string
    	path to YAML configuration file
  -dir string
    	source directory (default "./dump")
  -drop
    	drop each bucket before restoring it
  -dry-run
    	validate every file and report what would change; write nothing
  -fingerprint value
    	pin the server's certificate fingerprint, SHA256:<64 hex>, which the server prints when it starts
  -insecure
    	skip TLS certificate verification (local development only)
  -json
    	print one JSON result object to stdout instead of a text summary
  -no-users
    	skip users.json (not yet implemented — no-op; accepted for forward compatibility)
  -password string
    	root password (env: HOARDDB_ROOT_PASSWORD)
  -replace-users
    	replace an existing account's enabled state and roles from the dump (not yet implemented — no-op; accepted for forward compatibility)
  -user string
    	root username (env: HOARDDB_ROOT_USER)
  -users
    	recreate users.json if present (not yet implemented — no-op; accepted for forward compatibility) (default true)
  -with-credentials
    	restore password material for a same-installation restore (not yet implemented — no-op; accepted for forward compatibility)


